## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to implement a simple, offline-first persistence layer with Codable, basic CRUD in a SwiftUI app, and derive a user-facing metric (streak) from daily entries. It avoids external dependencies and targets beginner-friendly data modeling and UI work.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI, Codable, FileManager and Documents directory usage
- Data model design for Habit and DayEntry
- Derived metrics: computing daily streaks from consecutive entries
- Basic CRUD operations and crash-safe resume through persistent storage
- Lightweight test plan focusing on persistence and streak logic

## Code Example
```swift
struct DayEntry: Codable { let date: Date; let completed: Bool }
struct Habit: Codable { let id: UUID; var name: String; var colorHex: String; var entries: [DayEntry] }

let url = FileManager.default.urls(for: .documentDirectory, in: .userDomainMask).first!.appendingPathComponent("habits.json")
func save(_ habits: [Habit]) throws {
  let data = try JSONEncoder().encode(habits)
  try data.write(to: url)
}
func load() -> [Habit] {
  guard let data = try? Data(contentsOf: url) else { return [] }
  return (try? JSONDecoder().decode([Habit].self, from: data)) ?? []
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle time zones and daylight saving when computing streaks?
- How would you extend this to sync across devices, while preserving offline-first behavior?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a robust, real-time data layer that stays responsive under network variability, using concurrency primitives and local caching.

## Key Concepts

- Swift concurrency with async/await and TaskGroups
- Local cache using SQLite
- Delta-based merge with per-item versioning
- Conflict resolution and ordering guarantees
- Test strategy for latency, reordering, and memory pressure

## Code Example

```swift
struct Post { let id: String; var version: Int; var content: String }
struct Delta { let id: String; let version: Int; let content: String }

func merge(_ deltas: [Delta], into cache: inout [String: Post]) -> [Post] {
  for d in deltas {
    if var existing = cache[d.id] {
      if d.version > existing.version {
        existing.version = d.version
        existing.content = d.content
        cache[d.id] = existing
      }
    } else {
      cache[d.id] = Post(id: d.id, version: d.version, content: d.content)
    }
  }
  return Array(cache.values)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you detect and mitigate race conditions in the delta merge?
- How would you extend to multi-device sync and server-driven conflict resolution?

